<%- include('partials/head') %>
<%- include('partials/header') %>

<main id="main">

  <%- include('partials/page-hero', {
    heroImage: '/img/hero-team.webp',
    eyebrow: 'Our people',
    eyebrowIcon: 'team',
    heroTitle: 'The Cleaning Team Behind Every Zenvorali Job',
    heroIntro: 'Every person who cleans a Kansas City home or office under our name is a directly employed, background-checked, insured professional. No subcontractors, no gig platforms, no rotating strangers with your key. Here is who you are actually hiring.',
    heroPoints: ['Criminal background checks on every hire', 'Drug screening & identity verification', 'Paid training and a supervised probation period', '$2M liability and full workers’ compensation'],
    breadcrumbs: [{ name: 'About Us', href: '/about' }, { name: 'Our Team', href: '/team' }]
  }) %>

  <!-- ============ INTRO ============ -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <div class="prose prose--lead reveal">
        <p>
          The cleaning industry has a staffing problem that most companies hide: a large share of the
          work is done by people who are not employees at all. They are gig workers or informal
          subcontractors, paid per job, with no training, no background check and no insurance. That
          model is cheap for the company and risky for the client, and it is why so many people have had
          the experience of a cleaning service that was excellent once and unreliable thereafter.
        </p>
        <p>
          We do it the expensive way because it is the only way that works. Every Zenvorali cleaner is on
          our payroll. They pass a criminal background check, a drug screen and identity verification
          before their first day. They go through paid training on surface safety, chemical dilution,
          dwell times and material-specific technique, then a supervised probation period before they
          are trusted with a client's key. We carry workers' compensation for all of them, because if
          an uninsured cleaner is injured in your home the exposure can reach your household policy.
        </p>
        <p>
          That costs considerably more per hour than the alternative, and it is why our crews stay for
          years instead of weeks, why quality holds as we grow, and why we can offer a re-clean guarantee
          that we have never had to argue about.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ TEAM GRID ============ -->
  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <header class="section__head section__head--center">
        <p class="eyebrow reveal"><span class="eyebrow__icon"><%- icon('users', 15) %></span> Leadership &amp; crew leads</p>
        <h2 class="section__title reveal">Meet the Zenvorali Team</h2>
        <p class="section__lede reveal">The people accountable for the standard — reachable by name, not a generic inbox.</p>
      </header>

      <div class="team-grid stagger">
        <% team.forEach(function (m) { %>
          <article class="team-card reveal team-card--<%= m.accent %>">
            <div class="team-card__media">
              <img src="<%= m.image %>" alt="<%= m.imageAlt %>" width="440" height="520" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
                   srcset="<%= srcset(m.image) %>" sizes="(min-width: 1040px) 31vw, (min-width: 640px) 47vw, 92vw">
              <span class="team-card__initials" aria-hidden="true"><%= m.initials %></span>
            </div>
            <div class="team-card__body">
              <h3 class="team-card__name"><%= m.name %></h3>
              <p class="team-card__role"><%= m.role %></p>
              <p class="team-card__bio"><%= m.bio %></p>
              <ul class="team-card__facts">
                <% m.facts.forEach(function (f) { %><li><%- icon('check', 13) %> <%= f %></li><% }); %>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </article>
        <% }); %>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ VETTING PROCESS ============ -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <header class="section__head section__head--center">
        <p class="eyebrow reveal"><span class="eyebrow__icon"><%- icon('shield', 15) %></span> How we hire</p>
        <h2 class="section__title reveal">What Every Cleaner Goes Through Before Your Home</h2>
        <p class="section__lede reveal">Trust in a cleaning service should rest on documented process, not on a reassuring paragraph on a website. Here is exactly what we check.</p>
      </header>

      <ol class="hire-steps stagger">
        <% [
          { n: '01', t: 'Application & screening call', d: 'References verified, work history checked and a structured phone interview covering experience, reliability and comfort with pets and children.' },
          { n: '02', t: 'Criminal background check', d: 'A full background check run through a third-party provider, plus identity verification and a pre-employment drug screen. We do not make exceptions.' },
          { n: '03', t: 'Paid training programme', d: 'Surface safety, chemical dilution and dwell times, material-specific technique, our 48-point checklist and client confidentiality. Unpaid training produces untrained cleaners.' },
          { n: '04', t: 'Supervised probation period', d: 'New cleaners work alongside an experienced lead for a minimum number of jobs before working independently, with the lead accountable for quality throughout.' },
          { n: '05', t: 'Ongoing inspection & review', d: 'Quarterly reviews, rotating quality inspections by our QA lead and refresher training whenever a new product, finish or process is introduced.' }
        ].forEach(function (s) { %>
          <li class="hire-step reveal">
            <span class="hire-step__num"><%= s.n %></span>
            <div class="hire-step__body">
              <h3 class="hire-step__title"><%= s.t %></h3>
              <p class="hire-step__text"><%= s.d %></p>
            </div>
          </li>
        <% }); %>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ CREW MODEL ============ -->
  <section class="section section--why">
    <div class="container why">
      <div class="why__media reveal">
        <img src="/img/team-crew.webp" alt="Zenvorali Cleaning crew of two arriving at a Kansas City home in uniform"
             width="820" height="700" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
             srcset="<%= srcset('/img/team-crew.webp') %>" sizes="(min-width: 960px) 45vw, 92vw">
        <span class="why__hex" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      </div>

      <div class="why__content">
        <p class="eyebrow reveal"><span class="eyebrow__icon"><%- icon('team', 15) %></span> How our crews work</p>
        <h2 class="section__title reveal">Two People, One Named Lead, Every Visit</h2>
        <p class="reveal">
          Most of our jobs are worked by a two-person crew, because a pair can split wet and dry work
          efficiently — one taking the kitchen and bathrooms while the other works the living areas and
          bedrooms. That division is deliberate. It roughly halves the time on site compared to a single
          cleaner, and it keeps wet work from interfering with dry surfaces.
        </p>
        <p class="reveal">
          Every crew has a named lead who is accountable for the visit and the checklist. When you book a
          recurring plan, your home is assigned to a specific crew that stays with it. Over a few visits
          that team learns everything without being told — where the products live, which surfaces are
          delicate, that the dog is friendly but the cat is not, and that the fourth stair tread needs
          the crevice tool.
        </p>

        <ul class="why__list stagger">
          <% [
            { icon: 'clock', t: 'Two-hour arrival windows', d: 'With a text when your crew is 20 minutes out — no all-day waiting.' },
            { icon: 'users', t: 'Consistent team assignment', d: 'Your home keeps the same crew on recurring plans, every time.' },
            { icon: 'key', t: 'Secure key handling', d: 'Keys logged in and out, coded storage, and alarm procedures signed for.' },
            { icon: 'clipboard', t: 'Uniformed and identifiable', d: 'Branded uniforms and ID on arrival, so you always know who is at the door.' }
          ].forEach(function (a) { %>
            <li class="why__item reveal">
              <span class="why__icon"><%- icon(a.icon, 22) %></span>
              <span><strong><%= a.t %></strong><span><%= a.d %></span></span>
            </li>
          <% }); %>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ HOW THE CREW WORKS ============ -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <div class="prose reveal">
        <h2 class="section__title">How a Zenvorali Crew Is Put Together</h2>
        <p>
          A cleaning company&rsquo;s quality is decided long before anyone picks up a cloth — it is decided
          by how crews are staffed, briefed and paid. Getting those three things wrong is why so many
          clients describe their previous cleaner as &ldquo;good at first, then it slipped&rdquo;. Here is the
          structure behind every visit we make.
        </p>
        <ul class="tick-list">
          <li><strong>Two cleaners, one named lead.</strong> Residential jobs run as a two-person crew with a designated lead who is accountable for the checklist and the final walkthrough. You get to know both names, not just a company.</li>
          <li><strong>Fixed routes, not random assignment.</strong> The same crew returns to the same homes wherever the schedule allows. Familiarity with a property is a genuine quality advantage — nobody has to rediscover where things are or how the client likes them done.</li>
          <li><strong>A briefing before, a walkthrough after.</strong> The lead reviews the scope notes before starting and walks the property at the end against the same checklist. Nothing is closed out until the lead has checked it.</li>
          <li><strong>Paid time, not rushed time.</strong> We build an honest duration estimate into the price and give the crew that time. A job squeezed into half the hours it needs is how corners get cut quietly.</li>
          <li><strong>Direct employment throughout.</strong> Every cleaner is on our payroll with workers&rsquo; compensation. We do not subcontract residential work, which means the person in your home is someone we hired, trained and can hold to a standard.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 class="section__title section__title--spaced">Training, in Detail</h2>
        <p>
          Every cleaner completes a structured paid induction before working unsupervised. It covers
          surface identification and what each material will and will not tolerate; chemical dilution and
          the dwell time each product needs to actually work; colour-coded microfiber discipline to prevent
          cross-contamination between restrooms and kitchens; the forty-eight point room checklist; safe
          ladder and extension-pole use; and client confidentiality, including what may and may not be
          discussed about a property.
        </p>
        <p>
          Training does not stop at induction. Crew leads run refreshers, new products are introduced with
          a demonstration rather than a memo, and every complaint or re-clean is reviewed with the crew
          involved so the same miss does not repeat. Several of our current leads started with us as
          cleaners, which is the clearest evidence we can offer that the training and progression paths are
          real rather than a recruiting line.
        </p>

        <h2 class="section__title section__title--spaced">Who Is Not on This Page</h2>
        <p>
          You will notice the team page shows a small number of people rather than a wall of stock
          photographs. That is deliberate. We only list people you might realistically speak to or meet —
          the leads and the office contacts who answer the phone. Cleaners who prefer not to be photographed
          and named are not listed, which is their choice and one we respect.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you want to know who is coming to your property before the first visit, ask. We will tell you
          the names of the crew assigned to you and, for recurring clients, we keep it consistent wherever
          scheduling allows.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

        <h2 class="section__title section__title--spaced">What We Look For When Hiring</h2>
        <p>
          Hiring well is the whole business, so it is worth being specific about what we screen for and what
          we screen out. Plenty of applicants can clean a room; far fewer can do it consistently, in someone
          else&rsquo;s home, to a documented standard, without being supervised every visit.
        </p>
        <ul class="tick-list">
          <li><strong>Reliability above raw speed.</strong> A fast cleaner who arrives late twice a month costs us more than a steady one. We check references specifically on attendance and punctuality.</li>
          <li><strong>Comfort in occupied homes.</strong> Most of our residential work happens with clients present or a key held in trust. Discretion, courtesy and working around a household are non-negotiable.</li>
          <li><strong>Willingness to follow the checklist.</strong> Our method is documented for a reason — the forty-eight point list exists because it catches the things people skip when they improvise.</li>
          <li><strong>Physical stamina for a full shift.</strong> Cleaning is genuinely physical work. We would rather be honest about that at interview than have someone discover it in week two.</li>
          <li><strong>A driving record and reliable transport.</strong> Our crews travel between properties across the metro, so a valid licence and dependable vehicle are requirements rather than preferences.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 class="section__title section__title--spaced">Accountability Between Visits</h2>
        <p>
          A named crew lead is accountable for each job, but accountability does not stop when the van
          leaves. Every visit is logged against the scope, complaints are reviewed with the crew involved
          rather than filed, and recurring quality issues surface in the quarterly review we run with
          commercial accounts. For residential clients, the same information reaches you simply — if a room
          was missed, the re-clean happens and the crew is retrained on that step.
        </p>
        <p>
          We also rotate supervision deliberately. A supervisor visits each crew unannounced at least
          monthly and works alongside them rather than observing from the doorway. It is the only way to see
          what a routine visit actually looks like when nobody is expecting an inspection, and it is how we
          catch drift before a client does.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ WORKING WITH US ============ -->
  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <div class="prose reveal">
        <h2 class="section__title">Working With Us as a Client</h2>
        <p>
          What a client actually experiences rarely matches what a company describes about itself, so here
          is what the first months of working with us look like in practice — including the parts that
          require something from you.
        </p>
        <p>
          On your first booking we send a short confirmation listing the agreed scope, arrival window and
          any access instructions. The crew lead reads those notes before starting. You will get a
          completion note after the visit, and on recurring plans you will get the same lead wherever
          scheduling allows. If you work from home or are present during the clean, the crew will work
          around you rather than asking you to rearrange your day.
        </p>
        <p>
          Two things genuinely help us do better work. First, tell us about anything delicate, antique,
          sentimental or easily damaged — a family piece, an unusual stone, a finish you are unsure about.
          We will test on an inconspicuous area rather than assume. Second, tell us your priorities. If the
          kitchen matters more than the spare room, say so and we will weight the time accordingly rather
          than dividing it evenly and leaving both half-done.
        </p>

        <h2 class="section__title section__title--spaced">If Something Goes Wrong</h2>
        <p>
          Eventually something will. A crew member will miss a surface, a booking will be moved, or a
          product will not suit a particular finish. What matters is what happens next, and our process is
          deliberately simple: tell your account lead or the office directly, and we return and put it right
          at no charge under the guarantee.
        </p>
        <p>
          We do not contest re-cleans or ask you to demonstrate the fault. If you say a room was missed, it
          was missed. The only thing we ask is that you tell us within forty-eight hours, because a surface
          we cleaned a fortnight ago is difficult to assess fairly and we would rather fix a genuine miss
          than argue about an unclear one.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ CAREERS TEASER ============ -->
  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container guarantee-band__inner reveal" style="background:var(--surface)">
      <span class="guarantee-band__icon" style="color:var(--accent)"><%- icon('team', 44) %></span>
      <h2 class="guarantee-band__title" style="color:var(--ink)">We Are Usually Hiring in Kansas City</h2>
      <p class="guarantee-band__text" style="color:var(--muted)">Direct employment, paid training, steady schedules, above-market pay and genuine progression. If you take pride in doing a job properly, we would like to hear from you.</p>
      <div class="guarantee-band__actions">
        <a class="btn btn--primary btn--lg" href="/careers">See Open Roles <%- icon('arrowRight', 18) %></a>
        <a class="btn btn--outline btn--lg" href="mailto:<%= site.email %>">Send Your CV</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <%- include('partials/cta', {
    ctaTitle: 'Ready to Have a Team You Can Rely On?',
    ctaText: 'Book a free walkthrough and meet the crew that would be assigned to your home or facility. No obligation, no pressure.'
  }) %>
</main>

<%- include('partials/footer') %>
<button class="to-top" id="toTop" type="button" aria-label="Back to top"><%- icon('chevronDown', 22) %></button>
